 ],
 "literal": "When thinking, it possesses wonder / resolving this here with such great glory / more than that, it possesses wonder, of another country's / noble man filled with eminence, cherishing / fame as tradition, friendship as support pillar / coming here at such a time / and the greatness of the king who said 'he will come' / and the wisdom of him who came without failure / every time one wonders, every time one wonders, it transcended wonder / therefore, dwelling in the land where his sceptre does not rule / the ancient fame obtained by touching the heart of the noble man / this world which lost such a person / what will it become, oh, it is indeed pitiable!",
 "translation": "When I think of it, it is filled with wonder: that he resolved to do this here with such great glory. But even more wondrous is how a noble man of eminent stature from another land, cherishing fame as his tradition and friendship as his pillar of support, came here at such a time. The greatness of the king who confidently said, 'He will come,' and the wisdom of the one who came without fail—as I wonder at it again and again, it transcends all wonder! Therefore, this world, having lost such a person who won the heart of the noble king and earned ancient fame even while living in a land where the king's sceptre did not rule, what will become of it? It is indeed a pitiable thing!",
 "modern": "நினைத்துப் பார்க்கும்போது வியப்பாக இருக்கிறது. இவ்வளவு பெரிய பெருமையோடு இங்கு உயிர் துறக்கத் துணிந்தது வியப்பானது. அதைவிட வியப்பானது, வேறு நாட்டில் வாழும் சிறந்த பண்பாளரான பிசிராந்தையார், புகழைத் தன் மரபாகவும் நட்பைத் தன் பற்றுகோலாகவும் கொண்டு, இத்தகைய துன்ப காலத்தில் இங்கு வந்தது. 'அவர் வருவார்' என்று உறுதியாகக் கூறிய அரசனின் பெருமையும், அதற்குப் பழுது வராமல் வந்து சேர்ந்த அந்தப் புலவரின் அறிவும், வியக்க வியக்க வியப்பின் எல்லையைக் கடந்து செல்கிறது. அதனால், தன் செங்கோல் செல்லாத வேறு நாட்டில் வாழ்ந்தாலும், அரசனின் நெஞ்சில் இடம்பிடித்துப் பழமையான புகழைப் பெற்ற அந்தச் சான்றோனை இழந்த இந்த உலகம் இனி என்ன ஆகுமோ? மிகவும் இரங்கத்தக்கது.",
 "explanation": "The poet Pottiyar is lamenting the tragic loss of King Kopperuncolan and his soul-friend, the poet Pisirandaiyar. Both had resolved to fast to death, a practice known as vadakkiruttal, to show their ultimate commitment to honor and friendship. Pottiyar marvels at how Pisirandaiyar, who lived in a different kingdom beyond the Chola king's rule, traveled all the way to join his friend in death. The king had confidently predicted his arrival, and the poet fulfilled this expectation without fail. This poem belongs to the potuviyal tiṇai and kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai, capturing the profound grief of losing such noble souls. The key words include maruṭkai (wonder), naṭpu (friendship), and cāṉṟōṉ (noble man).",
